 },
 "brief": {
  "short_answer": "FAA guidance requires attempting to reestablish the control link and, if unsuccessful, initiating emergency or contingency procedures for loss of control link. It also requires preflight control link loss procedures in the briefing, and that if VLOS cannot be regained, predetermined procedures (including possible return-to-home or landing) are followed. Transfers of control between remote pilots are allowed only when both can maintain VLOS without LOC. Overall, guidance emphasizes control-link checks, predefined loss procedures, and maintaining or regaining VLOS as soon as practicable.",
  "sections": [
   {
    "section": "faa",
    "label": "FAA guidance",
    "status": "drafted",
    "abstain_reason": null,
    "claims": [
     {
      "text": "FAA guidance instructs to try to reestablish the control link with the small unmanned aircraft, and if the connection cannot be reestablished, to start emergency or contingency procedures for loss of control link.",
      "citations": [
       "faa_ac_107_2a_2022_c0228"
      ],
      "quote": "Example: Try to reestablish control link connection with the small unmanned aircraft. If connection cannot be reestablished, start emergency or contingency procedures for loss of control link.",
      "conditions": [
       "None"
      ],
      "assessment": "supported",
      "claim_id": "C001",
      "section": "faa",
      "tier": "official",
      "incident_label": null
     },
     {
      "text": "Preflight briefing must include control link loss procedures, and risk assessment/mitigation steps should include describing what happens if the loss of control link occurs.",
      "citations": [
       "faa_ac_107_2a_2022_c0227"
      ],
      "quote": "The preflight briefing must include control link loss procedures.",
      "conditions": [
       "None"
      ],
      "assessment": "supported",
      "claim_id": "C002",
      "section": "faa",
      "tier": "official",
      "incident_label": null
     },
     {
      "text": "Part 107 allows transfer of control of a small UAS between certificated remote pilots only if both are capable of maintaining Visual Line of Sight without loss of control.",
      "citations": [
       "faa_ac_107_2a_2022_c0032",
       "faa_ac_107_2a_2022_c0033"
      ],
      "quote": "Two or more certificated remote pilots transferring operational control (i.e., the remote PIC designation) to each other may do so only if they are both capable of maintaining Visual Line of Sight (VLOS) of the small unmanned aircraft without loss of control (LOC).",
      "conditions": [
       "None"
      ],
      "assessment": "supported",
      "claim_id": "C003",
      "section": "faa",
      "tier": "official",
      "incident_label": null
     },
     {
      "text": "If the remote PIC cannot regain VLOS, they should follow pre-determined procedures for the loss of VLOS, with actions depending on the small UAS and environment (e.g., land, hover, or return-to-home).",
      "citations": [
       "faa_ac_107_2a_2022_c0049"
      ],
      "quote": "If the remote PIC cannot regain VLOS, the remote PIC or person manipulating the controls should follow pre-determined procedures for the loss of VLOS. The capabilities of the small UAS will govern the remote PIC's determination as to the appropriate course of action. For example, the remote PIC may need to land the small unmanned aircraft immediately, enter hover mode, or employ a return-to-home sequence.",
      "conditions": [
       "None"
      ],
      "assessment": "supported",
      "claim_id": "C004",
      "section": "faa",
      "tier": "official",
      "incident_label": null
     }
    ]
   },
